    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, our lineup construction prioritizes high-floor output against vulnerable pitching, with **Taylor Ward** leading the charge at a 71.46 efficiency. **Taylor Ward** benefits immensely from the platoon advantage against Lodolo and a favorable venue, while **Bryson Stott** (71.06) remains a high-end start; despite a slight penalty for the opponent\u2019s ability to suppress stolen bases, **Bryson Stott**\u2019s elite positioning against the high xERA of Avila makes him a cornerstone. We also see massive upside in **Lawrence Butler** (70.17), whose power profile is amplified by a +10% park factor, making him a critical engine for our HR and RBI production. Given our focus on the scarcity of SB, keep a close eye on **Jung Hoo Lee** (59.53) and **Daulton Varsho** (53.89); both benefit from favorable stolen base environments created by slow-delivery pitchers, and while they fall below the \"Strong\" start threshold, their speed profile justifies their inclusion to keep us competitive in the category.\n\nThe toughest decision on the bench involved **Gavin Sheets**, who posted a 47.41 score against Sheehan. While **Gavin Sheets** offered a solid platoon edge, he was ultimately edged out by the superior consistency and lineup placement of our primary outfielders. The rest of our bench, including the veteran **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez**, **Will Smith**, and **Tyler Freeman**, remains sidelined due to injury or rest, leaving us with limited pivot options should any late-scratch lineup news emerge. We are currently navigating a thin roster, but with **Cal Raleigh** (64.20) providing a steady presence behind the plate and **Luis Arraez** (62.57) maximizing his leadoff opportunity, we are well-positioned for an efficient day. Be mindful of **Geraldo Perdomo**, whose 58.26 score is dampened by a matchup against the tougher arm of Sproat, but he remains essential for maintaining our SB pace.",
